# ECDH-ES agreement and JWE in Livebook

This notebook runs P-256, P-384, and X25519 key agreement, encrypts compact JWEs to public recipient keys, and decrypts through caller-owned custody handles. It covers direct ECDH-ES and both AES key-wrap variants from [RFC 7518 §4.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7518.html#section-4.6), with X25519 as specified by [RFC 8037 §3.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8037.html#section-3.2). It uses real local cryptography and needs no network peer beyond the Hex install.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:request_seal, "~> 2.0"}])
```

## Check the published X25519 example

The following scalar and public key are public test material from [RFC 8037 Appendix A.6](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8037.html#appendix-A.6), also used in [RFC 7748 §6.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7748.html#section-6.1). They are never production credentials. The custody handle must derive the published public bytes; unsupported X25519 fails the cell instead of skipping it.

```elixir
alias RequestSeal.Custody
alias RequestSeal.JOSE.{JWE, KeyManagement}

scalar = Base.decode16!("77076D0A7318A57D3C16C17251B26645DF4C2F87EBC0992AB177FBA51DB92C2A")
published_public = Base.url_decode64!("hSDwCYkwp1R0i33ctD73Wg2_Og0mOBr066SpjqqbTmo", padding: false)

{:ok, vector_handle} = Custody.Local.new({:jwe, "ECDH-ES"}, {:x25519, scalar})

try do
  {:ok, %{material: {:x25519, ^published_public}}} = Custody.public_key(vector_handle)
after
  :ok = Custody.release(vector_handle)
end

IO.puts("Published X25519 public bytes reproduced")
```

## Create recipients for all three curves and every agreement mode

Each handle binds one JOSE algorithm and grants only `:unwrap`. The sender receives its public key; generation happens inside the sensitive holder. Each curve and mode gets its own fresh key pair.

```elixir
recipients =
  for curve <- ["P-256", "P-384", "X25519"],
      algorithm <- ["ECDH-ES", "ECDH-ES+A128KW", "ECDH-ES+A256KW"] do
    {:ok, handle} = Custody.Local.generate({:jwe, algorithm}, curve: curve)
    [:unwrap] = handle.capabilities
    {:ok, public} = Custody.public_key(handle)
    %{curve: curve, algorithm: algorithm, handle: handle, public: public}
  end

9 = length(recipients)
IO.puts("Nine algorithm-bound recipients ready")
```

## Inspect the agreement primitive

`KeyManagement.agree/4` accepts decoded party information. Direct mode derives the content-encryption key (CEK); the key-wrap modes derive a wrapping key and wrap a fresh CEK. Both calls below must produce fresh ephemeral public keys and CEKs. The primitive returns CEK bytes to its caller, so this cell checks their width without displaying them. Envelope consumers can use `JWE.encrypt/3`, as the next cell does, to keep CEKs inside library workers.

```elixir
for recipient <- recipients do
  {:ok, first} =
    KeyManagement.agree(recipient.algorithm, "A256GCM", recipient.public, %{
      apu: "sender",
      apv: "recipient"
    })

  {:ok, second} =
    KeyManagement.agree(recipient.algorithm, "A256GCM", recipient.public, %{
      apu: "sender",
      apv: "recipient"
    })

  for agreement <- [first, second] do
    32 = byte_size(agreement.cek)
    ["epk"] = Map.keys(agreement.header)
    false = Map.has_key?(agreement.header["epk"], "d")
    curve = recipient.curve
    ^curve = agreement.header["epk"]["crv"]

    case recipient.algorithm do
      "ECDH-ES" -> "" = agreement.encrypted_key
      _ -> 40 = byte_size(agreement.encrypted_key)
    end
  end

  false = first.cek == second.cek
  false = first.header == second.header
end

IO.puts("Fresh agreement and public-only epk checked for all nine recipients")
```

## Encrypt to public keys and decrypt through custody

The compact format is [RFC 7516](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7516.html). Its protected header contains Base64url-encoded party information and only the public `epk`. Direct ECDH-ES has an empty encrypted-key segment; the key-wrap modes carry a wrapped CEK. The resolver returns a trusted handle under an explicit algorithm, cipher, context, size, and deadline policy. Header context is public and is not authorization.

```elixir
policy_for = fn handle ->
  {:jwe, algorithm} = handle.algorithm

  %{
    algorithms: [algorithm],
    encryption: ["A256GCM"],
    max_plaintext: 1024,
    timeout: 5_000,
    resolve_timeout: 1000,
    unwrap_timeout: 1000,
    protected: %{expected: %{"kid" => "notebook-recipient"}, permitted: []},
    key_resolver: fn _ -> {:ok, %{algorithm: algorithm, key: handle}} end
  }
end

envelopes =
  for recipient <- recipients do
    {:ok, token} =
      JWE.encrypt(
        [
          {"alg", recipient.algorithm},
          {"enc", "A256GCM"},
          {"kid", "notebook-recipient"},
          {"apu", Base.url_encode64("sender", padding: false)},
          {"apv", Base.url_encode64("recipient", padding: false)}
        ],
        "an encrypted reading",
        recipient.public
      )

    [_protected, encrypted_key, _iv, _ciphertext, _tag] = String.split(token, ".")

    case recipient.algorithm do
      "ECDH-ES" -> "" = encrypted_key
      _ -> 40 = byte_size(Base.url_decode64!(encrypted_key, padding: false))
    end

    {:ok, opened} = JWE.decrypt(token, policy_for.(recipient.handle))
    "an encrypted reading" = opened.plaintext
    algorithm = recipient.algorithm
    ^algorithm = opened.algorithm
    "A256GCM" = opened.encryption
    curve = recipient.curve
    ^curve = opened.header["epk"]["crv"]
    false = Map.has_key?(opened.header["epk"], "d")
    true = opened.header["apu"] == Base.url_encode64("sender", padding: false)
    true = opened.header["apv"] == Base.url_encode64("recipient", padding: false)
    Map.put(recipient, :token, token)
  end

9 = length(envelopes)
IO.puts("All nine envelopes authenticated and decrypted")
```

## Reject altered content, malformed public keys, and wrong custody

The mutation changes an actual ciphertext byte while keeping valid Base64url. A separate mutation makes `epk` malformed. A trusted handle on the wrong curve fails with the same crypto error as tampering, and an algorithm outside the policy is refused at input.

```elixir
for envelope <- envelopes do
  policy = policy_for.(envelope.handle)
  [protected, encrypted_key, iv, ciphertext, tag] = String.split(envelope.token, ".")
  <<first, rest::binary>> = Base.url_decode64!(ciphertext, padding: false)
  changed = Base.url_encode64(<<Bitwise.bxor(first, 1), rest::binary>>, padding: false)

  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}} =
    JWE.decrypt(Enum.join([protected, encrypted_key, iv, changed, tag], "."), policy)

  header = :json.decode(Base.url_decode64!(protected, padding: false))
  malformed = put_in(header, ["epk", "x"], "")

  malformed_protected =
    malformed |> :json.encode() |> IO.iodata_to_binary() |> Base.url_encode64(padding: false)

  {:error, %{reason: :invalid_header, layer: :input}} =
    JWE.decrypt(Enum.join([malformed_protected, encrypted_key, iv, ciphertext, tag], "."), policy)

  other =
    Enum.find(recipients, fn recipient ->
      recipient.algorithm == envelope.algorithm and recipient.curve != envelope.curve
    end)

  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}} =
    JWE.decrypt(envelope.token, policy_for.(other.handle))

  {:error, %{reason: :algorithm_not_permitted, layer: :input}} =
    JWE.decrypt(envelope.token, %{policy | algorithms: ["RSA-OAEP-256"]})

  {:error, %{reason: :duplicate_member, layer: :input}} =
    JWE.encrypt(
      [{"alg", envelope.algorithm}, {"enc", "A256GCM"}, {"epk", %{}}],
      "payload",
      envelope.public
    )

  {:error, %{reason: :invalid_header, layer: :input}} =
    KeyManagement.agree(envelope.algorithm, "A256GCM", envelope.public, %{
      apu: "same",
      apv: "same"
    })
end

IO.puts("Every curve and mode rejected tampering, malformed epk, wrong custody, and policy errors")
```

## Release the caller-owned keys

```elixir
for recipient <- recipients do
  :ok = Custody.release(recipient.handle)
  {:error, %{reason: :key_not_found}} = Custody.public_key(recipient.handle)
end

IO.puts("All recipient handles released")
```

Successful JWE decryption establishes recipient integrity, not sender identity. These local round trips and the published public-key check do not establish deployed-peer acceptance. Continue with the [JOSE guide](../docs/guides/jose.md), [key custody](../docs/guides/key-custody.md), and [testing and evidence](../docs/guides/testing.md).
